-- js-shapes.rofl — THE THIRD AXIS, as facts.
--
-- The two-axis matrix is (language, node KIND, layer) and it has a blind spot
-- that this file exists to open up. `member_expression` is one kind and six
-- different jobs — `o.m()`, `a.b.c()`, `f().g()`, `this.x()`, `super.x()`,
-- `o[k]()` — and the matrix carries ONE tick for all of them. The execution
-- oracle in test/js-callgraph.test.ts found 37 edges where the model found 26,
-- and six of the eleven misses sit inside that one ticked cell. A table built
-- so that an EMPTY cell shouts said nothing, because the hole was inside a
-- FULL one.
--
-- Nothing here is asserted about JavaScript. Every row is a statement about
-- the MODEL, contradictable by a rule in rules/js-model.rofl.
--
-- PROVENANCE OF THE SHAPE LIST — it is measured, not invented. The shapes and
-- the kind each one belongs to are read off the classification tables in
-- rules/js-callgraph.rofl (`callee_shape`, `member_shape`, and the three
-- computed-key rules), and the list is cross-checked in
-- test/js-model.test.ts against the CENSUS the rules actually produce on the
-- call corpus: 83 call sites, 17 distinct shapes. The corpus is the work
-- order and the grammar is the denominator, so a shape nobody has written yet
-- still has a cell here.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE AXIS. Declaring it is NOT what makes it exist — a relation has a fixed
-- arity, so an axis is a COLUMN and the column was cut in rules/js-model.rofl
-- by hand. What this row buys is that `axis_applies` can be checked against
-- it, and that the set of axes is enumerable rather than implied.
axis(shape).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHERE THE AXIS APPLIES, and this is the row the whole design turns on.
--
-- ONE LAYER, not two. The draft of this file carried `axis_applies(shape,
-- dataflow)` as well, on the argument that a callee's shape matters to data
-- flow too. `unearned_axis[audit]` refused it, and the refusal is correct:
-- nothing is modelled at shape granularity in the dataflow layer, so every one
-- of the thirty shaped cells it would open carries the same verdict and the
-- same reason as its neighbour. That is 30 rows of arithmetic and 0 questions,
-- which is exactly the pathology the applicability row exists to prevent —
-- measured one tick earlier as 24 kinds x 2 layers = 48 cells, not one of them
-- a question.
--
-- ...AND THAT REFUSAL WAS ABOUT A DIFFERENT `shape_in` SET, 2026-09-08
-- (w_meta_property). It was measured with the CALLEE-shape vocabulary in the
-- dataflow column, where every one of the thirty cells carries its neighbour's
-- answer; the refusal is still correct about those thirty. `axis_applies(shape,
-- dataflow)` is declared in facts/js-dataflow.rofl now, with exactly two values
-- gated into that layer by `shape_in` — `new_target` and `import_meta` — and
-- they carry DIFFERENT irreducible reasons, so `axis_earns[audit]` has a
-- witness there rather than thirty rows of arithmetic. The applicability row is
-- what makes that checkable in either direction, which is what this paragraph
-- said it was for.
--
-- There is deliberately NO row for `modules`. A callee's shape is a fact about
-- a call and says nothing whatever about a module graph; `importKind` is the
-- local axis there and it is not this one. That absence is not a silence — it
-- is the difference between a CROSS-CUTTING axis and a LOCAL one, and it is
-- checkable: declare the row and `unearned_axis[audit](shape, modules)` says
-- no.
axis_applies(shape, callgraph).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE KINDS THE AXIS NAMES THAT NOBODY HAD DECLARED. Three, and they are the
-- price of the column: a shape whose kind is absent from `node_kind` is an
-- `orphan_shape[audit]`, the same vocabulary hole `orphan_claim` was written
-- for, reopened by a new relation over the same arguments exactly as
-- rules/js-model.rofl predicts each new relation will.
--
-- All three are callee positions the grammar allows and this corpus does not
-- contain, which is why the call-graph model never had to name them: the
-- pseudo-kind babel gives `import(...)` in callee position, a parenthesised
-- callee, and a TS `as`-expression callee.
node_kind(js, import).
node_kind(js, parenthesized_expression).
node_kind(js, tsas_expression).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SHAPE OF KIND. 30 rows over 15 kinds, covering 25 of the 26 shapes in
-- `shape_vocab[audit]`.
--
-- THE ONE THAT CANNOT BE WRITTEN, declared rather than left silent:
-- `s_unclassified` is the catch-all, reached exactly when a callee's kind is
-- in no table, so it belongs to NO kind and has no cell in this matrix. The
-- axis is keyed by kind and the catch-all is keyed by the absence of one. That
-- is a third structural limit of the same family as the two in
-- docs/modelling-a-language.md, and the shape ledger in
-- facts/js-callgraph.rofl covers it while this matrix cannot.

-- one kind, one shape: the callee IS the form
shape_of(js, identifier,                s_identifier).
shape_of(js, function_expression,       s_iife).
shape_of(js, arrow_function_expression, s_iife).
shape_of(js, super,                     s_super).
shape_of(js, tsnon_null_expression,     s_non_null).
shape_of(js, call_expression,           s_call_result).
shape_of(js, optional_call_expression,  s_call_result).
shape_of(js, new_expression,            s_new_result).
-- THE SECOND ROLE OF THE SAME KIND, added 2026-09-04 with the constructor edge.
-- `s_new_result` is `new C()` standing in the CALLEE position — `(new C())()`.
-- `s_construct` is `new C()` doing what it usually does: constructing. Two
-- roles, one kind, and the refinement column is what tells them apart — which
-- is the same reading the modules layer already gave it, where the values are
-- specifier shapes rather than callee shapes. Without this there is nowhere to
-- record the constructor edge at all: the kind is split, so it has no unrefined
-- cell, and the coarse `unknown_because` it used to carry became unreachable.
shape_of(js, new_expression,            s_construct).
-- ADDED 2026-09-05 with the generator protocol. A yield expression in CALLEE
-- position is its own shape and it RESOLVES, so the kind is split and its
-- verdict moves to the shape — the same construction `new_expression` has, and
-- for the same reason: the kind means two different things at this layer.
shape_of(js, yield_expression,          s_yield_result).
shape_in(s_yield_result, callgraph).
shape_in(s_construct, callgraph).
handled(js, new_expression, s_construct, callgraph, r_construct).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE THIRD KIND THE COLUMN SPLITS, 2026-09-08 (w_meta_property) — and the
-- first whose two values are not two ROLES of one construct but TWO CONSTRUCTS
-- wearing one node kind. `new.target` is ES2015 and `import.meta` is ES2020,
-- babel gives both `meta_property`, and the scanner has always emitted the
-- discriminator: zero `ast_attr` rows and two identifier children, so
-- (meta="new", property="target") against (meta="import", property="meta").
-- `meta_form[code]` in rules/js-structure.rofl is the one relation that reads
-- it; `child_needs` in facts/js-env.rofl reads the same child for the ERA gate
-- and is keyed on a feature, so it cannot answer a question about the FORM.
--
-- THE ITEM'S NOTE SAID THIS COULD NOT BE DONE AND THE ROW IT CITED IS ONE FILE
-- OVER. It reads "the shape axis that could split it applies to `callgraph`
-- only (facts/js-shapes.rofl)", pointing at the paragraph above that declines
-- `axis_applies(shape, dataflow)`. Measured: `axis_applies(shape, modules).`
-- has been in facts/js-modules.rofl since the modules layer had shapes, and the
-- column's whole design is ONE COLUMN WITH PER-LAYER VOCABULARIES — `shape_in`
-- is what keeps a specifier shape out of the call graph. So the split was
-- available at two of the three layers before this item started, and the third
-- costs one `axis_applies` row, declared beside its values in
-- facts/js-dataflow.rofl.
shape_of(js, meta_property, new_target).
shape_of(js, meta_property, import_meta).
shape_in(new_target, callgraph).
shape_in(import_meta, callgraph).

-- AS A CALLEE, `new.target` IS THE CONSTRUCTOR OF THE CURRENT INVOCATION, which
-- is a fact about the CALL and not about the text — the taxonomy's own
-- `runtime_dependent`, and the second row in this repository to carry it.
-- MEASURED rather than reasoned: in `class Derived extends Base {}`, `new.target`
-- inside `Base`'s constructor is `Derived` under `new Derived()` and `Base`
-- under `new Base()`; in a plain call it is `undefined`; and `new new.target()`
-- reports the enclosing function as its caller (`Recur -> Recur`), so unlike an
-- await this one is an ordinary edge whose CALLEE is undecidable. A rule
-- pointing it at the enclosing function would be an under-report the moment a
-- subclass exists, and the corpus cannot even discriminate the question:
-- measured, 246 call targets and 14 transfer targets with an EMPTY intersection,
-- so no function here is both called and constructed.
unknown_because(js, meta_property, new_target, callgraph, runtime_dependent).
-- `import.meta` IS NEVER A CALLEE. `import.meta()` is a TypeError, and the only
-- call-graph position it occupies is the RECEIVER of a member call — which is
-- `member_expression x s_member_on_meta` and answered there, with the site
-- `import.meta.resolve(spec)` in shapes.ts. Waived here rather than left as
-- backlog, because there is no form to model.
ignored(js, meta_property, import_meta, callgraph, a_import_meta_is_never_a_callee).
shape_of(js, import,                    s_dynamic_import).
shape_of(js, sequence_expression,       s_sequence).
shape_of(js, parenthesized_expression,  s_parenthesized).
shape_of(js, tsas_expression,           s_ts_as).
shape_of(js, conditional_expression,    s_conditional).

-- THE KIND THIS FILE EXISTS FOR. Thirteen shapes: ten receivers a static
-- member callee can have, plus the three computed-key forms. Nine of the
-- thirteen occur in the corpus; the other four are grammar and are here for
-- the same reason facts/js-callgraph.rofl writes verdicts for shapes it never
-- saw — the denominator is the grammar.
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_ident).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_member).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_call).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_this).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_super).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_array).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_new).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_object_literal).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_literal).
-- EIGHT SPLIT OUT OF THE CATCH-ALL, 2026-09-04. `s_member_on_other` carried
-- every object position the classifier did not name, and a probe with one site
-- per position showed the bucket holding FOUR different answers at once: four
-- forms that already resolved, two that needed one value rule each, one owned
-- by the control-form item and one that is the standard library. A single cell
-- reported `not_yet` for all of them.
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_conditional).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_sequence).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_logical).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_assignment).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_await).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_template).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_cast).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_non_null).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_meta).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_member_on_other).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_computed_literal_key).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_computed_dynamic_key).
shape_of(js, member_expression, s_computed_template_key).

-- `a?.b()` has its own kind and its own shape when the access is static; when
-- it is computed it lands in the same three computed forms, because the rules
-- read `computed` before they read optionality.
shape_of(js, optional_member_expression, s_optional_member).
shape_of(js, optional_member_expression, s_computed_literal_key).
shape_of(js, optional_member_expression, s_computed_dynamic_key).
shape_of(js, optional_member_expression, s_computed_template_key).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT IS MODELLED, PER SHAPE. Four rows, and the number is the finding.
--
-- The kind-level ledger in facts/js-callgraph.rofl carries twelve `handled`
-- rows at the call-graph layer. Six of them name a kind this axis splits, and
-- those six tick NOTHING once the cells they named stop existing — they are
-- reported by `coarser_claim[audit]` rather than deleted, because each is
-- still true of some shape and false of the rest. What survives the refinement
-- is these four: the identifier callee (tier 1), the IIFE in its two spellings
-- (tier 1b), and `ns.method()` where `ns` is a local object literal (tier 2).
--
-- `r_call_edge` for all four, because one rule set is what was written and
-- inventing four rule ids would be the tautology rules/js-model.rofl already
-- paid for.
handled(js, identifier,                s_identifier,       callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, function_expression,       s_iife,             callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, arrow_function_expression, s_iife,             callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, member_expression,         s_member_on_ident,  callgraph, r_call_edge).

-- THREE MORE TICKED 2026-09-04, by ONE rule rather than three. `denotes` in
-- rules/js-callgraph.rofl answers `what object does this expression denote`,
-- and the shapes below are entry points into it rather than separate cases:
-- `a.b.c()` is the relation calling itself, `this.m()` is a class method's
-- receiver, `o?.m()` needed no rule at all because the member kinds were
-- already both in the vocabulary. `r_denotes` is a NEW rule id and not
-- `r_call_edge`, because a cell's rule id is what a reader follows to find out
-- WHY the cell is ticked, and these three are ticked by different reasoning.
handled(js, member_expression,          s_member_on_member, callgraph, r_denotes).
handled(js, member_expression,          s_member_on_this,   callgraph, r_denotes).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, s_optional_member,  callgraph, r_denotes).

-- THREE MORE, 2026-09-04, and only one of them cost a rule. `s_member_on_super`
-- needed `super_of` in the dataflow pack — `super` denotes the class ABOVE the
-- one you are in. `s_member_on_new` and `s_member_on_object_literal` needed
-- nothing at all: they were unmodelled because the CORPUS had no site, and two
-- sites in shapes.ts showed the rules had covered them all along.
-- A TEMPLATE AS A COMPUTED KEY, closed 2026-09-08 when the scanner's contract
-- grew one property. No rule was written for it: `selects[flow]` already reads
-- a computed key through `may_be_lit`, and a template with no interpolation is
-- a string written the other way. Both member kinds close on the same arm.
handled(js, member_expression,          s_computed_template_key, callgraph, r_template_key).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, s_computed_template_key, callgraph, r_template_key).
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_super,          callgraph, r_denotes).
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_new,            callgraph, r_denotes).
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_object_literal, callgraph, r_denotes).

-- SIX MORE, 2026-09-04, and the split is what made the verdicts differ.
-- FOUR NEEDED NOTHING BUT A NAME — the value layer already saw through a
-- conditional, a sequence and both TypeScript wrappers, so the sites resolved
-- the moment the classifier stopped lumping them together.
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_conditional, callgraph, r_denotes).
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_sequence,    callgraph, r_denotes).
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_cast,        callgraph, r_denotes).
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_non_null,    callgraph, r_denotes).
-- TWO COST ONE VALUE RULE EACH, in rules/js-dataflow.rofl. `a || b` is the
-- same may-set as `c ? a : b` and was invisible next to it; `(x = o)` has the
-- value of its right-hand side, and ONLY for a plain `=` — the operator guard
-- is the difference between an assignment and a sum.
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_logical,     callgraph, r_value_logical).
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_assignment,  callgraph, r_value_assign).

-- `super()` THE CALL, 2026-09-04 (queue item w_cg_call_result). The callee's
-- value is the parent class and what it reaches is that class's constructor,
-- walking up the extends chain past any class that declares none. The residue
-- — a chain with no explicit constructor anywhere — is `no_source_target` and
-- is recorded on the FRONTIER in facts/js-callgraph.rofl, which is where a
-- reason may stand beside a tick.
handled(js, super, s_super, callgraph, r_super_ctor).

-- ...and the dynamic-import shape WAIVED, because this parser cannot produce a
-- site for it at all. The reason is in facts/js-callgraph.rofl beside the
-- frontier row it replaces.
ignored(js, import, s_dynamic_import, callgraph, a_babel_emits_no_import_callee).

-- THE SYNTACTIC WRAPPERS, 2026-09-04, and all of them by ONE rule rather than
-- five: `resolves[code](C, F) :- callee_of(C, N), may_be_node[flow](N, F),
-- fn_node(F)` asks what the callee expression may BE instead of recognising how
-- it is spelled. The dataflow layer already sees through a cast, an assertion,
-- a sequence and a conditional, so the call graph did not have to learn any of
-- them — and the next wrapper the grammar grows will need no rule here either.
handled(js, tsnon_null_expression,  s_non_null,     callgraph, r_value_callee).
handled(js, tsas_expression,        s_ts_as,        callgraph, r_value_callee).
handled(js, sequence_expression,    s_sequence,     callgraph, r_value_callee).
handled(js, conditional_expression, s_conditional,  callgraph, r_value_callee).
handled(js, call_expression,          s_call_result, callgraph, r_value_callee).
handled(js, optional_call_expression, s_call_result, callgraph, r_value_callee).

-- AND ONE WAIVER, WITH A MEASUREMENT BEHIND IT. `s_parenthesized` has no site
-- and never will: babel does not emit ParenthesizedExpression under this
-- parser configuration, so `(f)(1)` reaches the classifier as a plain
-- identifier callee. The shape was declared from the GRAMMAR and the parser is
-- not the grammar — the same gap docs/modelling-a-language.md records as
-- `babel is not the language`. If the scanner ever turns that option on, a site
-- appears and this waiver goes stale, which is the property a reason should
-- have.
ignored(js, parenthesized_expression, s_parenthesized, callgraph, a_babel_emits_no_parenthesized_node).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TYPED REASONS, only where the default `not_yet` would be a LIE. Same
-- convention as facts/js-kinds.rofl: every other not_modelled shape takes
-- `not_yet` from the rule, and writing the defaults out would make the
-- taxonomy look exercised without exercising it.
--
-- `o[k]()` with a non-literal k names a method that does not exist until the
-- program runs. This is the ONE irreducible cell of the whole axis, and
-- nothing else on the list may borrow the reason: `a.b.c()` is merely
-- unfinished, and `o[`fixed`]()` is knowable at parse time and blocked on the
-- scanner's scalar-only contract, which is ours.
-- THESE TWO CELLS WERE TICKED 2026-09-04 AND THEIR REASON MOVED. `o[k]()` used
-- to be `runtime_dependent` at CELL level — the one irreducible verdict this
-- layer spent — and the value layer then resolved two of its three sites: a
-- const key in the same file, and a literal passed as an argument. A cell with
-- resolved sites is `handled`; what stays unknowable is the RESIDUE, and the
-- residue is where `runtime_dependent` belongs. `bag[key]` with an ambient
-- `key` is still irreducible and is recorded as such in facts/js-callgraph.rofl.
--
-- This is f_runtime_dependent_is_a_verdict_about_a_shape_and_both_its_sites_are_decidable
-- coming true, and it was found by an audit rather than by remembering.
handled(js, member_expression,          s_computed_dynamic_key, callgraph, r_value_key).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, s_computed_dynamic_key, callgraph, r_value_key).
handled(js, member_expression,          s_computed_literal_key, callgraph, r_value_key).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, s_computed_literal_key, callgraph, r_value_key).
handled(js, member_expression,          s_member_on_call,       callgraph, r_denotes).

-- the receiver is a host literal and its methods belong to the runtime rather
-- than to any corpus this analyser can be pointed at
unknown_because(js, member_expression, s_member_on_array, callgraph, out_of_scope).
-- ...and `"str".toUpperCase()`, which answers through the same built-in
-- prototype. REVERTED TO not_yet 2026-09-04: I wrote out_of_scope here and
-- scope is the owner's word. The neighbour above carries out_of_scope from
-- before this branch and is left exactly as it was — changing somebody else's
-- recorded decision on a guess is the same overreach in the other direction.
-- `scope_unowned[audit]` names both, and it names them until they are settled.
-- ATTRIBUTED, AND STILL EDGELESS — w_env_api_surface, 2026-09-08, and the two
-- halves of that sentence are the whole result. `stdlib_member[audit]` names
-- the residue; `lib_call[code]` in rules/js-env-api.rofl now says WHICH library
-- method it is and SINCE WHEN, from facts/js-lib-surface.rofl, which
-- scanners/ts_lib.ts generates from TypeScript`s own lib.es*.d.ts — 130 members
-- of eight prototypes, each dated by the earliest lib file that declares it.
--
-- SO THE REASON CHANGES FROM `not_yet` TO `no_source_target`, which is a move
-- from OURS to IRREDUCIBLE and takes the cell out of the work queue. That is
-- not a waiver: the call really does transfer control, into `Array.prototype.join`,
-- and there is no node in this program for an edge to reach. What was `not_yet`
-- meant `somebody could still do something here`, and after this item nobody
-- can — every rule that could be written has been, and the remaining gap is
-- that the target has no source.
--
-- WHAT THE ITEM DELIVERED INSTEAD OF AN EDGE is an ANSWER at the era layer:
-- `lib_unsupported[audit]` reports that `[1,2,3].at(0)` is invalid under es5,
-- es2015, es2016 and es2020 and valid under ts5, that `'a'.replaceAll` is
-- invalid under everything up to es2020, and that `1n.toString()` is invalid
-- below es2020 — three methods, three different years, discriminated. That is
-- the half of the environment question the item`s own note called `what syntax
-- cannot answer`.
unknown_because(js, member_expression, s_member_on_literal, callgraph, no_source_target).

-- TWO OF THE SPLIT ARE OPEN AND EACH IS OWNED SOMEWHERE ELSE, which is the
-- point of splitting them out rather than leaving them in a bucket whose
-- verdict would have to be one word for both.
--
-- `(await p).m()` CLOSED 2026-09-04 by the item that owned it: `await` became
-- transparent to the value layer and the site resolved with no member rule
-- touched. `shape_stale[audit]` named the excuse the same run, which is the
-- healthy direction for an excuse to rot in.
handled(js, member_expression, s_member_on_await, callgraph, r_await_transparent).
-- `` `k${n}`.concat() `` — the receiver is a String and `concat` answers
-- through the built-in prototype. Not a value in this program at all; it is
-- the standard library, which is `w_env_api_surface`.
unknown_because(js, member_expression, s_member_on_template, callgraph, no_source_target).

-- AND THE CATCH-ALL ITSELF IS NOW EMPTY, WHICH IS ITS JOB. Every object
-- position the probe found has a name of its own, so `s_member_on_other` holds
-- nothing — and it must not read `not_yet`, because there is no form to model:
-- the shape is the CATCHER, and a catcher with something in it is news rather
-- than a backlog item. `catch_all_occupied[audit]` in rules/js-callgraph.rofl
-- is that news, and this waiver is what stops the queue offering work whose
-- subject nobody has seen. The pair is the same construction `s_unclassified`
-- already has one level up, and it is the honest version of the `absent` that
-- `s_parenthesized` showed means two different things.
-- `import.meta.resolve(spec)` — THE HOST'S MEMBER AND NOT THE LANGUAGE'S,
-- 2026-09-08 (w_meta_property). Same atom as the two above and a different
-- reason for it: those two reach a method the standard library declares and
-- this model cannot see, and `lib_call[code]` names them from
-- `lib.es*.d.ts`. This one reaches a method NO `lib.es*` file declares at all —
-- measured, `interface ImportMeta` in `lib.es5.d.ts` is empty and `resolve`
-- lives in `lib.dom.d.ts` and `@types/node`, which `releaseOf` refuses by
-- construction. So `lib_call` is silent here on purpose and
-- `stdlib_unattributed[audit]` stays silent too, because the receiver has no
-- `builtin_prototype`: it is not a prototype the language owns.
unknown_because(js, member_expression, s_member_on_meta, callgraph, no_source_target).

ignored(js, member_expression, s_member_on_other, callgraph, a_catch_all_empty_by_design).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHICH LAYER EACH VALUE BELONGS TO. The column is shared across layers and
-- its vocabulary is not: every value below is a CALLEE shape, and a callee
-- shape is a fact about a call. facts/js-modules.rofl declares its own values
-- for the same column, and `shape_in` is what keeps the two sets from minting
-- each other's cells.
shape_in(s_call_result, callgraph).
shape_in(s_computed_dynamic_key, callgraph).
shape_in(s_computed_literal_key, callgraph).
shape_in(s_computed_template_key, callgraph).
shape_in(s_conditional, callgraph).
shape_in(s_dynamic_import, callgraph).
shape_in(s_identifier, callgraph).
shape_in(s_iife, callgraph).
shape_in(s_member_on_array, callgraph).
shape_in(s_member_on_call, callgraph).
shape_in(s_member_on_ident, callgraph).
shape_in(s_member_on_literal, callgraph).
shape_in(s_member_on_member, callgraph).
shape_in(s_member_on_new, callgraph).
